WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.520
Researchers have developed a blood test that detected breast cancer at an early stage.

00:05.520 --> 00:09.920
It also flagged the risk of the disease coming back after treatment.

00:09.920 --> 00:12.960
So, how can you see all that in blood?

00:14.240 --> 00:19.360
When cancer is just starting, its traces in the blood are extremely rare.

00:19.360 --> 00:22.960
They are hard to find without also raising false alarms,

00:22.960 --> 00:26.240
flagging cancer in a woman who doesn't have it.

00:26.240 --> 00:31.200
In a joint study, Professor Efrat Shema of the Weizmann Institute of Science

00:31.200 --> 00:35.760
and Professor Guy Ron of the Hebrew University went looking for a specific

00:35.760 --> 00:41.840
signature that tumors leave behind. To decode it, they started with the tumors themselves.

00:42.480 --> 00:48.480
Dr. Noa Furth, a researcher on the team, brought samples from surgery to the lab on ice

00:48.480 --> 00:54.080
and testing began the same day. Why the rush? Once a tumor is removed,

00:54.080 --> 01:00.320
the marks they were after survive only briefly. Those marks are called epigenetic tags.

01:00.880 --> 01:06.160
Think of DNA as a recipe book. The tags are the sticky notes, telling the cell

01:06.160 --> 01:12.240
which recipes to use and how much. In cancer cells, those instructions can go wrong too.

01:13.200 --> 01:18.800
When cells die, they release fragments of DNA wrapped around proteins into the blood.

01:19.440 --> 01:22.720
The researchers managed to read the tags on those fragments

01:22.720 --> 01:25.440
and search a blood sample for the tumor's signature.

01:26.400 --> 01:30.640
They compared blood samples from women with breast cancer and healthy women

01:30.640 --> 01:38.000
and trained AI models to tell them apart. The study reports 95% accuracy in early detection

01:38.000 --> 01:41.760
when the tumor is still local and no larger than 2 cm.

01:42.960 --> 01:47.680
Then came a second discovery. Even in tumors considered low risk,

01:47.680 --> 01:52.880
a rare group of aggressive, invasive cells was hiding with a signature of its own.

01:53.760 --> 01:58.240
The more common that signature was in the blood, the higher the risk of relapse.

01:58.960 --> 02:03.520
Combining it with other markers, the researchers told apart patients whose cancer

02:03.520 --> 02:09.360
returned during follow-up from those who stayed disease-free with 81% accuracy.

02:10.000 --> 02:14.480
They detected risk even in patients considered low risk by standard measures.

02:15.440 --> 02:19.200
Now they are looking for treatments that would block cancer cells' ability to

02:19.200 --> 02:23.920
change and turn more aggressive. The hope is that this research will also

02:23.920 --> 02:30.960
help prevent the disease from coming back. Follow for more research like this.

